/// FINDING (v-compiler-ml, 2026-07-16) — the MAP analogue of the now-fixed empty-Set-to-list divergence
/// is STILL OPEN: `Map.to-list(Map.empty)` — `cdz check` EXIT 0, but `cdz compile` FAILS
/// "Map.to-list key/value shape has no orderable descriptor". A checker-accepts / backend-rejects
/// divergence, same class as mlrepro-set-to-list-empty-literal-check-passes-compile-fails (which was
/// RESOLVED for Set.of([]) — but the fix did NOT cover the Map side).
///
///   def run() = List.len(Map.to-list(Map.empty))
///     cdz check → exit 0 (no diagnostic); cdz compile → "no orderable descriptor" (no CDZ code).
///
/// DISCRIMINATORS (verified 2026-07-16):
///   • Set.of([]) + Set.to-list — now COMPILES + runs (0). The Set side IS fixed.
///   • Map.empty + Map.to-list — STILL diverges (check 0 / compile error). The Map side is NOT.
///   • A runtime-empty but key/value-TYPED map — Map.to-list(Map.remove(Map.insert(Map.empty,1,10),1)) —
///     COMPILES + runs (0). So it is the UNDETERMINED key/value type of the bare `Map.empty`, exactly as
///     the Set fix handled the undetermined element type of `Set.of([])`.
///
/// So the Set fix (baking a default/ordering descriptor for an undetermined element type, or rejecting it
/// at check-time with a clear coded diagnostic) needs the SAME treatment for Map.empty's undetermined
/// key type. Low severity (a typed-empty map works; annotating fixes it), but it is the remaining half of
/// a reject-don't-diverge violation. The queue item mlrepro-set-to-list-empty-literal-check-passes-
/// compile-fails.RESOLVED closed only the Set half.
def run() = List.len(Map.to-list(Map.empty))
export { run }
